          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"alcott-little-women-7061a6f40caee479\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Mr. Brooke directly avows that he loves Meg. The utterance primarily presents his loving state as true. Its courtship setting gives it slight undertaking force because it helps initiate a possible future engagement, but the words themselves do not clearly constitute a promise or commitment. Tender and emotionally charged delivery does not provide independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited T/P ambiguity: the declaration is part of a marriage-oriented courtship exchange, but its core force remains an avowal rather than a definite relational undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Mr. Brooke directly addresses Meg and says, “I love you so much, dear,” while asking whether she cares for him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration occurs during a deliberate courtship conversation. Brooke seeks reciprocal affection and later offers to wait while Meg learns whether she can love him, framing the avowal as persuasive and future-oriented. Meg is emotionally affected and ambivalent, while the narrator later notes Brooke’s confidence in his success. These features qualify the interaction but do not displace the utterance’s dominant truth-conditional force or establish reflex-like expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I only want to know if you care for me a little, Meg. I love you so much, dear,”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal and places it within a request for reciprocal affection.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says Mr. Brooke added the declaration “tenderly.”\",\"supports\":\"This establishes affectionate delivery but, without evidence of blurting or affective loss of control, does not independently support expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Brooke subsequently asks, “Will you try and find out?” and says he needs to learn whether he is “to have my reward in the end or not.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration functions within sustained, purposeful courtship, giving slight support to relational undertaking force while remaining principally an avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Brooke offers, “I’ll wait,” while Meg invokes her youth and her father’s view that it is too soon.\",\"supports\":\"A future commitment is articulated elsewhere in the exchange, showing why the highlighted declaration has some performative context but does not itself bear the full commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.93}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core utterance: it is overwhelmingly an avowal, with slight courtship-related undertaking force and no independently evidenced expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
